# max_atoms capacity sweep: identical to in.si_bench except that the timed run
# is the SECOND run, so XLA compilation and the first neighbour-list build are
# outside the measurement.
#
# in.si_bench times a single `run`, which carries the one-off compile inside the
# loop time -- tolerable when comparing sizes, not when the whole question is a
# few-percent slope against max_atoms, and compile cost may itself move with
# capacity.  Here `run ${warm}` pays for both, then `run ${steps}` is timed.
#   -var reps N     cells per side (6 -> 1728 atoms)
#   -var bundle     path to the .lammps-jax.json under test
#   -var pjrt       PJRT plugin .so
#   -var newt       on | off -- the `newton` command AND the -pk kokkos setting
#                   must agree, so both are driven from this one variable.
#                   pair jax/kk rejects a mismatch against the bundle contract.
variable reps  index 6
variable warm  index 5
variable steps index 50
variable newt  index on

units metal
atom_style atomic
boundary p p p
newton ${newt}

lattice diamond 5.43
region box block 0 ${reps} 0 ${reps} 0 ${reps}
create_box 1 box
create_atoms 1 box
mass 1 28.0855
displace_atoms all random 0.05 0.05 0.05 12345 units box

neighbor 1.0 bin
neigh_modify every 1 delay 0 check yes one 2000 page 2000000

pair_style jax/kk ${pjrt}
pair_coeff * * ${bundle}

velocity all create 300.0 87287 loop geom
fix 1 all nve
timestep 0.0
thermo 1000
thermo_style custom step atoms pe
thermo_modify norm no format float %.10g
run_style verlet/kk
run ${warm}
run ${steps}
